<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Lithuania (1918)</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1918 CE · capital Vilnius</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived monarchy declared by the Council of Lithuania in 1918, which elected a German prince as King Mindaugas II.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> recognised Lithuanian independence in March 1918 on condition of close ties with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire</a>, and plans circulated to unite the crown with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. To avoid this, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-lithuania">Council of Lithuania</a> on 11 July 1918 proclaimed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> and invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-karl-duke-of-urach">Prince Wilhelm of Urach</a>, a Catholic from Wurttemberg, to be king.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Wilhelm accepted and took the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-karl-duke-of-urach">Mindaugas II</a>, after the medieval king of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-lithuania">Kingdom of Lithuania</a>, but he never came to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a> and the German authorities did not recognise his election. In practice the country remained under German military occupation, while the council prepared Lithuanian institutions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany's</a> defeat approached, the Council revoked the election on 2 November 1918 and adopted provisional republican principles. The state continued as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-lithuania-1918-1940">Republic of Lithuania</a> (1918-1940). Wilhelm never set foot in the country, and the episode is remembered as a curiosity of Lithuanian state-building.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-lithuania-1918.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Lithuania (1918)" width="320" height="309"><figcaption>Image: SeriousThinker · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Lithuania_(1251%E2%80%931263).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vilnius</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mindaugas II (Wilhelm of Urach)</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Lithuanian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/lithuania">Lithuania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Lithuania of Mindaugas II, Kingdom of Lithuania, Lietuvos Karalystė</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=kingdom-of-lithuania-1918" aria-label="Kingdom of Lithuania (1918) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5226 300 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Lithuania (1918) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5546 556L5300 622M5546 556L5526 556M5546 556L5526 548"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5300 622h0M5526 556h0M5526 548h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5546 556h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5546 556h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=kingdom-of-lithuania-1918">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
